## HTML semantic tags, their importance, and how to structure HTML documents effectively. It builds upon prior knowledge of HTML basics, focusing on the evolution from using generic <div> tags to more meaningful semantic elements that improve both readability and maintainability of web pages.

### Key Insights and Core Concepts

# **HTML Tag Diversity**:
    
HTML offers a vast range of tags, far beyond the basic ones. Some examples include:
    
- `<code>`: Used to display programming code snippets (e.g., JavaScript, PHP, Python, Java).
- `<kbd>`: Represents keyboard input, useful for showing commands or shortcuts.
- `<pre>`: Displays preformatted text, preserving whitespace and line breaks.

### Examples

<code>console.log("Hello")</code>
<kbd>Ctrl + C</kbd>
<pre>
This text
keeps spacing
and line breaks
</pre>

# **Importance of Semantic Structure**:
    
Earlier web development practices heavily relied on generic `<div>` containers with classes to segment page parts like header, navigation bar, content, and footer. This approach, while functional, lacked clarity and semantic meaning.

❌ Old Approach
<div class="header"></div>
<div class="nav"></div>
<div class="content"></div>
<div class="footer"></div>
    
# **Modern Semantic Tags**:

✅ New Approach
<header></header>
<nav></nav>
<section></section>
<article></article>
<aside></aside>
<footer></footer>

# Purpose: Clearly defines page structure and Improves readability

To improve clarity and ease of maintenance, HTML introduced semantic tags such as:
    
- `<header>`
- `<nav>`
- `<section>`
- `<article>`
- `<aside>`
- `<footer>`
    
These tags explicitly define the role of each section in the document, making it easier for developers and browsers to understand the structure.
    
# **Advantages of Semantic Tags**:
- Easier to read and maintain code.
- Simplifies targeting elements with CSS and JavaScript.
- Enhances accessibility and SEO since browsers and search engines better understand document structure.

### OR

- Easier to read & maintain code
- Better CSS & JavaScript targeting
- Improves SEO & accessibility
- Helps browsers understand content

# **No Absolute Right or Wrong**:
    
Using semantic tags is not mandatory. Developers can still use `<div>` and classes, and both practices are valid depending on context and preference.

- Semantic tags are not mandatory
<div> can still be used and Both approaches are valid
    
# **Continual Updates in HTML**:
    
HTML is continuously evolving, with semantic tags being one of the recent important additions. Tags like `<figure>` and `<figcaption>` are part of the modern semantic set and help describe media content clearly.

# New Tags
<figure>
  <img src="image.jpg" alt="Example">
  <figcaption>Image Description</figcaption>
</figure>

